 <section xml:id="atomic-data_numeric-data">
         <title>Numeric Data</title>
         <p>Numeric C++ data types include <c>int</c> <idx><c>int</c></idx> for integer, <c>float</c> <idx><c>float</c></idx>
             for floating point, <c>double</c> <idx><c>double</c></idx> for double precision floating point.</p>
         <p>The standard arithmetic operations, +, -, *, and /
             are used with optional parentheses to force the order of
             operations away from normal operator precedence.</p>
         <p>These operations work just as they do in Python. The only difference is that integer division
             is truncated. So we have to be careful with division. For example 5/2 is still 2
             i.e. When two integers are divided, integer division is used.
             To get the whole quotient, declaring one of the numbers as a float will
             convert the entire result into floating point.</p>
         <p>Exponentiation in C++ is done using <c>pow()</c> <idx><c>pow()</c></idx> from the <c>cmath</c> <idx><c>cmath</c></idx> library
             and the remainder (modulo) operator is done with <c>%</c>.</p>